21:32:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Because a videogame is usually built around the specific hardware, operating system, tools, and rules of the platform it was made for. “Copying and pasting” may work for some platform-independent game logic, but not for the whole game. Common reasons include: - **Different processor architectures:** A game compiled for one console’s CPU generally cannot run directly on another console’s CPU. - **Different graphics systems:** Platforms use different graphics APIs and GPU capabilities. Code written for DirectX, for example, may need changes to work with Vulkan, Metal, OpenGL, or a console’s proprietary graphics API. - **Different operating-system services:** File access, networking, audio, user accounts, achievements, cloud saves, notifications, and permissions all work differently. - **Different input hardware:** A keyboard and mouse game needs adaptation for controllers; a touchscreen game needs different controls; consoles may have motion sensors, touchpads, or platform-specific buttons. - **Different performance limits:** One platform may have less memory, a slower CPU, or a weaker GPU. Developers may need to optimize textures, effects, loading, physics, or level design. - **Different screen and device setups:** Phones come in many screen sizes; PCs have varied hardware; consoles have TV-focused interfaces and certification requirements. - **Platform rules and certification:** Console and mobile storefronts require games to handle pausing, disconnects, parental controls, error messages, privacy rules, accessibility, and other platform-specific behavior. - **Third-party libraries and licenses:** A game may rely on software, middleware, codecs, or services that are unavailable or licensed differently on the target platform. If the game was designed with a portable engine such as Unity or Unreal, much of the core gameplay code and assets can often be reused. But developers still need to rebuild it for the new platform and modify the platform-specific parts. It is usually more accurate to call this **porting** than completely rewriting.
